This repository is two things under one roof, so it carries two licences.

  The software  — MIT, in full below.
  The content   — Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International,
                  in full in LICENSE-CONTENT.

Which is which
--------------

MIT covers the program that builds the site:

  build.mjs             as a program: the generator, its helpers and the
                        client-side JavaScript it emits
  src/schema.mjs        the data schema and its validator
  src/diagrams.mjs      and the SVG line art it draws, including the files
                        written to docs/assets/gamesir/
  src/assets/           the stylesheet, the favicon and og-image.png
  src/og-image.html     the source artwork that og-image.png is captured from
  serve.mjs             the preview server

CC BY-SA 4.0 covers the wiki itself:

  data/controllers.json every specification, documented fix, FAQ answer and
                        source record
  the page prose        the human-readable copy on the generated pages, which
                        for prose pages is held as strings inside build.mjs
  docs/*.html           the generated pages, as compilations of the above

docs/sitemap.xml and docs/robots.txt are neither: they are lists of addresses,
carrying no expression worth licensing.

build.mjs is deliberately listed twice, because it genuinely holds both: the
code is MIT, and the sentences that code prints are CC BY-SA. If you are
reusing the generator, take the code. If you are reusing what the site says
about a controller, take the content and attribute it.

Neither licence grants any trademark rights. "GameSir" and the product names
used throughout are trademarks of their owner, and this project is not
affiliated with, endorsed by, or operated by them. No GameSir photography,
render or marketing asset is reproduced here; the site links to official pages
instead. Third-party sources cited on the site remain the property of their
publishers — the citations are references, not redistribution.

Contributions are accepted under these same terms. Opening a pull request
means offering your change under MIT if it is code and under CC BY-SA 4.0 if
it is content, which is what keeps the wiki redistributable by anyone.


